"""The Author's clean-room gate (spec 000 FR-041; plan §11, §19; ADR 0017; research.md R-23, R-35, R-36).

    tools/gates/gate [--host] [--prove] [--otlp-url URL] REF
    tools/gates/gate --contract-replay FILE

Run it directly from main's checkout, never through make: a planted GNUmakefile would shadow the Makefile.
The branch's code never runs outside a container in the Author's terminal. On the host the gate runs only git, docker,
gitleaks, and the main working tree's own code: this file, tools/gates/*.py, and deploy/agents-engine/engine.sh, each
with an environment it builds itself; and, loaded into this process, so in its environment, corpus/tools/
check_corpus.py, evals/agent-config/behavioral.py, the static.py it loads beside it, and the PyYAML both import, from
the host's own Python. The commit under test is data there.

That code is main's only in main's checkout, so the gate refuses to start (exit 2) unless HEAD is refs/heads/main, the
tracked files match it in the index and the working tree, git is told to hide no change (assume-unchanged,
skip-worktree), and no file git doesn't track, ignored or not, sits in tools/gates/, corpus/tools/, evals/agent-config/
(its history/ aside), or deploy/agents-engine/, but Python's bytecode caches, which PY_HOST never reads. With a branch
checked out, the branch's code would grade the branch. A branch that changes this file runs its own copy, which no
check in it can stop, so the Author runs main's. --contract-replay is held to the main working tree alone: make
contract-live reaches it through the checkout's own Makefile and operator.py, which have run by then.

1. Export. REF resolves once to a commit SHA, which every later step and the merge use. Every git call runs with
   GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS=1. A ref under refs/replace stops the gate, since the Author's merge would honor it. So do
   .git/info/attributes and submodules, which git archive would apply or skip, and any path git takes for its own
   directory (.git in any case, or as NTFS spells it: a file, a folder, or a link, anywhere in the tree), which git
   would read as a repository if anything ran it in the export. `git archive` exports the commit, tarfile's data
   filter extracts it, and the export must hash to the tree its commit object names, the object rehashed against
   its SHA. Anything short of that proof stops the gate. The export is read-only from then on.
2. Makefiles. The container phase's make would read a GNUmakefile or makefile before the Makefile, so the export may
   hold neither, or exactly main's GNUmakefile and no makefile. Anything else stops the container phase.
3. Container phase, on this machine's engine (plan §11: the devcontainer build needs Ubuntu's mirrors). The
   devcontainer image is built from the export's .devcontainer/ and tagged by that directory's tree hash. A copy of
   the export gets `npm ci --ignore-scripts`, with the network. Then, each with `--network none`: make lint,
   validate-config, test, evals, redteam-suite, matrix (stage H), and credits-check (spec 003's credits and records,
   SKIP for a branch from before them). Every container drops all capabilities, sets
   no-new-privileges, and runs as the Author's uid on that copy. When the export has the room (apps/web), the room is
   built last, without drafts, its scripts off, with main's two policy files mounted read-only at /policy
   (content-build; spec 002, T080), and the host then runs main's content_check.py over the export and that build's
   dist/ (content): it fails when the build publishes a content file main doesn't approve, by its own account or in
   any file's text. main's policy is read by the branch's full ref, refs/heads/main, never a tag of that name.
4. Host phase:
   - githooks: the installed hooks match the main tree's .claude/git-hooks/, core.hooksPath is unset,
     gc.packRefs is false, and main is a loose ref, as `make githooks-check` has it;
   - toolchain: tools/gates/toolchain_check.py against the export's pins; a Claude Code newer than its pin, a floor
     (ADR 0023), passes the check with a "WARN:" line, which makes the step WARN;
   - policy: tools/gates/compose_policy.py --root <export>;
   - corpus: corpus/tools/check_corpus.py over the export's corpus, with the Author's denylist, which must exist;
     loaded into this process, which runs with PY_HOST's flags, since the script reads only its own tree;
   - gitleaks over $(git merge-base main SHA)..SHA, with the main tree's .gitleaks.toml (--config), a fresh empty
